Q: following vue code will cause memory leak, but I don&#39;t know the reason A: <p>I think issue is here</p>
<pre><code>
onMounted(() =&gt; {
  if (httpIntervalId.value) {
    clearInterval(httpIntervalId.value)
    httpIntervalId.value = null
  }

  httpIntervalId.value = setInterval(() =&gt; {
    sendHttpRequest()
  }, 5000)
})
</code></pre>
<p>If the component remounts, the old interval ID is cleared but the interval itself might still be running if there was a timing issue.</p>

Q: Golang - convert [8]bool to byte A: <p>The potentially fastest generic solution using the multiplication technique:</p>
<pre><code>func ConvertToByte(mei [8]bool) uint8 {
    // Read the bool array as bytes
    byteArray := *(*[8]byte)(unsafe.Pointer(&amp;mei))
    result := binary.BigEndian.Uint64(byteArray[:])

    return uint8(0x0102040810204080 * result &gt;&gt; 56)
}
</code></pre>
<p>Another alternative:</p>
<pre><code>func ConvertToByte2(b [8]bool) uint8 {
    byteArray := *(*[8]byte)(unsafe.Pointer(&amp;b))
    result := binary.LittleEndian.Uint64(byteArray[:])

    return uint8(0x8040201008040201 * result &gt;&gt; 56)
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can find the explanation for this technique in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/51750902/995714">this question</a></p>
<p><a href="https://go.dev/play/p/6EbBjHeo2NA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo on go playground</a></p>
<hr />
<p>If you have a lot of values to convert then SIMD would be much better. <a href="https://go.dev/doc/go1.26#simd" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Go 1.26</a> is introducing a new <a href="https://go.dev/pkg/simd/archsimd/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>simd/archsimd</code> package</a> and you can do something like this to pack the bools as bits</p>
<pre><code>import (
    &quot;simd/archsimd&quot;
    &quot;unsafe&quot;
)

// This can be done for 16 or 32 bytes at once as well
func ConvertToByte(mei [8]bool) uint8 {
    var zero archsimd.Uint8x16
    byteArray := *(*[8]uint8)(unsafe.Pointer(&amp;mei))
    v := archsimd.LoadUint8x16SlicePart(&amp;byteArray)
    mask := v.Equal(zero)

    // Shift by 8 in this case because we only care about the first 8 bytes
    // But for 16/32 bytes we don't need to do that
    return uint8(mask.ToBits() &gt;&gt; 8)
}
</code></pre>
<p>The package was designed to be cross-platform, so it can run on ARM, RISC-V... efficiently. Unfortunately due to the portability it probably can't be as efficient as the best SSE2 solution on x86 as of now:</p>
<pre><code>// func pack8boolsSSE2(a *bool) uint8
TEXT ·pack8boolsSSE2(SB), NOSPLIT, $0-9
    MOVQ    a+0(FP), AX        // Load pointer to bool array
    MOVQ    (AX), X0           // Load 8 bytes into lower half of XMM0
    PSLLD   $7, X0             // Move least significant bit to most significant position
    PMOVMSKB X0, AX            // Extract high bit of each byte
    MOVB    AX, ret+8(FP)      // Store result as uint8
    RET
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>The best scalar solution on many platforms would be <a href="https://www.felixcloutier.com/x86/pext" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>PEXT</code></a> or the equivalent instruction like <code>BEXT</code> on RISC-V. Sadly the <a href="https://github.com/golang/go/issues/45455" rel="nofollow noreferrer">request to add <code>PEXT</code> intrinsic was rejected</a>. But in case there are some packages supporting <code>PEXT</code> in the future then it can be done like this</p>
<pre><code>result := binary.BigEndian.Uint64(byteArray[:])
return intrinsic.Pext(result, 0x0101010101010101);
</code></pre>
